{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). Aisha Khan (user_id 5, HR Generalist, aisha.khan@brightline.example, device BL-LT-0105) reports clicking the link in a payroll verification email and entering her password. Click time not given in the ticket; need to confirm with the user. Related message found in her quarantine: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I instead of l), subject \"Urgent: verify your payroll login\", received 2026-10-10 21:01 UTC, marked phishing and not releasable. The copy she clicked probably reached her inbox some other way, or there were several copies; please check mail flow for other recipients and block the sender/domain for everyone. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 sign-ins, all successful, all from New York (corporate 203.0.113.10 / residential 198.51.100.25), all within usual hours with Duo push, no hosting networks, no failures or denied MFA. Most recent is 2026-10-09 13:35 UTC, which is before the phishing email arrived. No suspicious activity found, so block_sign_in was NOT requested. Duo enrolled (push, her phone). Password reset queued (approval id 1); temp password to be given by phone after callback verification. Still needed: approve the reset, confirm the click time with the user and whether she approved any Duo push, revoke active sessions/tokens, check for new mailbox rules or forwarding, and block the sender domain tenant-wide.",
  "reason": "KB-029 and KB-008 say always escalate; ticket is P1"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us right away. Reporting it quickly is the best thing you could have done. We're treating your password as exposed. I've asked for it to be reset. Once a technician approves that, they will call you to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password over the phone (never by email). You'll then choose a new one. I've also passed this to our security team. I checked recent sign-ins to your account and didn't see anything unusual. In the meantime: 1) Please don't delete the email or forward it to anyone. Security may want to look at it. 2) Never approve a Duo prompt on your phone that you didn't start yourself. If you get one, deny it and let us know right away. 3) If you used the same password anywhere else, change it there too. If you remember roughly what time you clicked the link, or if you approved any Duo prompt around then, please reply and tell us.",
  "internal_note": "P1 phishing credential entry (KB-029, KB-008). User 5, Aisha Khan, HR, device BL-LT-0105. Quarantine has Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example, a lookalike domain, subject 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', received 2026-10-10 21:01 UTC, marked phishing and not releasable. Not released. The copy she clicked probably reached her inbox another way. Sign-in logs: 10 successful sign-ins from NY (corporate/residential IPs), all in usual hours with Duo push. No hosting networks or failures. Last sign-in 2026-10-09 13:35 UTC, which is before the phishing email arrived. No suspicious activity, so block_sign_in was not requested. Duo enrolled. reset_password queued (approval id 1). Deliver the temp password by phone after callback verification. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation id 1). Asked them to revoke sessions, check mailbox rules and forwarding, check other recipients, and block the domain. Click time is unknown; asked the user to tell us.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
